{"kind":"reasoning","id":"p7-acbc0afb","text":"Line 112 is the `const ext = /\.(woff|woff2|eot|ttf|otf)$/.exec(googleFontFileUrl)[1];` — this is line ~112. Hmm, `exec(...)` returns null when the URL doesn't end in a font extension. So a font file URL from the CSS doesn't match. That happens if findFontFilesInCss produced a URL weirdly — e.g., Google returned a CSS with `url(https://fonts.gstatic.com/l/font?kit=...)` for some fonts (the "l/font?kit=" API history variant). Indeed IBM Plex Sans via the `css2` API with an old UA can return `https://fonts.gstatic.com/l/font?kit=...woff2&skey=...` — that ends with `&skey=...`, not a font extension → exec returns null → reading [1] throws.

But wait, in this project the CSS is fetched with a standard UA... Hmm. Actually the known cause: Google returns legacy CSS for `display=swap`? Let me actually reproduce: run the loader's download the CSS with fetch as Next does (UA is `Mozilla/5.0 ...`)? Easier: fetch CSS with Next's UA and inspect URLs.

Also possible: the emitter receives a font file URL with `#` etc. Let's just fetch the CSS the way Next's fetch-css does. Look at filename fetch-css.js in @next/font dist to see UA used.

Rather than go deep: reproduce by writing a small script using the loader? The build already reproduces it. Let's just check what the fonts CSS contains when requested with the UA Next uses.

