The sync problems had four concrete causes, all in [deviceController.js](backend/controllers/deviceController.js) — plus an unindexed lookup in [model.js](backend/models/model.js). I fixed each one.

**1. A sync could hang forever (the “syncing does not work” case).** `zk.createSocket()` was awaited with no timeout. The library only arms a socket timeout, never listens for it, so if the device was off, unreachable, or still holding a previous session, that promise simply never settled. Because every device call shares one queue and `runSync` caches its promise, one stuck sync blocked *all* later syncs and device-status checks until you restarted the backend. Connections are now capped at 15s, and the socket is force-destroyed and its reference cleared if closing hangs; every attempt is guaranteed to settle.

**2. Empty device logs made sync slow or fail.** With zero records, `node-zklib` issues a zero-size chunk request and waits 10 seconds for a packet that never arrives; depending on firmware it then returns an empty body or a parse error. Sync now asks the device for its log count first (`getInfo().logCounts`, a cheap command): **0 logs → returns immediately** (`0ms` in the test) without downloading anything.

**3. An emptied database could fail the whole sync.** Every device log was inserted in one `bulkWrite`. After deleting rows, the next sync re-imports the device’s full history — thousands of upserts in a single command can exceed MongoDB’s 16 MB command limit or stall, so the sync failed after doing all the downloading. Writes are now batched at 500 documents, with duplicate-key races ignored so one duplicate can’t abort the batch.

**4. Partial reads were reported as success.** The library resolves with `{ data, err }`; `err` was ignored, so a packet timeout mid-transfer could store an incomplete set while reporting “done”. A read error now triggers a retry and nothing partial is saved.

Supporting changes: retries now wait 3s then 6s (a device needs a moment to free the dropped session) and the final error reads like `TIMEOUT WHEN RECEIVING PACKET (after 3 attempts)`; abandoned in-flight promises no longer produce unhandled rejections; and `{ device: 1, timestamp: -1 }` is indexed so the “latest stored punch” lookup stops scanning and sorting the whole collection on a large database.

**Verified with a fake device** (scratch test, removed afterwards):
- Device with 0 logs → `{logs:0, written:0, deviceLogs:0}` in 1 ms, no attendance download.
- 1,200 device logs against an empty DB → 3 batched writes, all 1,200 inserted with correct `userId`/`timestamp`.
- Partial-read error → 3 attempts, rejected after ~11s, **0** documents written.
- `createSocket()` that never resolves → gives up after ~17s with `Device connection timeout` and closes its sockets, instead of hanging.

Not tested against the real ZKTeco device — the fake verifies the control flow, timeouts and batching, not the device’s own responses. Also note the physical limit remains: when the device holds a large history, sync must still transfer all of it (the protocol has no “only new” read), so the *first* sync after wiping the database will still take time proportional to the log count, just without the failure.
